<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Round Orange.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Label common items using a model to copy from. Interactive: Write simple phrases with errors on a drawing or postcard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can label food items in drawings with simple words (e.g., writing "round" under a picture of an orange). Can write simple phrases with guidance, describing food shapes (e.g., "This is a square cracker."). Can copy simple sentences about food shapes with teacher support (e.g., copying the word "juice box" under a picture of a juice box).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of familiar food items with distinct shapes (e.g., an orange, a cracker, a banana, a juice box). The teacher asks, "What is this?" and "What shape is it?" to review vocabulary and shapes. Students respond chorally. Then, the teacher shows simple written labels for each food item. For example, the teacher shows a picture of an orange and the word "orange".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes the words "round" and "cracker" clearly on the board, demonstrating correct letter formation for 1st graders. The teacher points to each letter as they write it, saying the sound (e.g., "/r/ in 'round'"), then the whole word. The teacher models writing "This is a round orange." and "This is a square cracker." The teacher emphasizes the /r/ sound in "round" and the /k/ sound in "cracker" while writing them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the orange square or round?" (Students point to the correct word on the board or say "round.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you see the letter 's' in 'square'?" (Students identify the letter, reinforcing phonemic awareness for writing.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated chart or a large poster showing various food items (orange, cracker, banana, lemon, juice box) with their names and a simple sentence describing their shape. Genre: Illustrated food labels/descriptions. Register: Simple descriptive language. Format: Picture chart with words and sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying food shapes: Identifying and describing foods by shape (e.g., "The cracker is square.") using written labels and simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Writing-focused):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: "Match the Word to the Picture."</b> The teacher displays the chart. Students are given a worksheet with pictures of food items and a list of words (e.g., "orange," "cracker," "banana," "round," "square"). Students draw a line from the correct word to its matching picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Circle the Shape Word."</b> Students look at the chart. On their worksheet, under each food picture, there are two shape words (e.g., "round / square" under an orange). Students circle the correct shape word that describes the food item.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing: Using adjective + noun (e.g., "round apple"), present simple (e.g., "Oranges are round."), and demonstratives ("This is a...") with target vocabulary (pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, food, juice box, snack, meal, eat, cut, hold, see, mix, round, square, oval, big, small, healthy, tasty, orange, black, brown, white).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: "Trace and Copy Shapes & Foods." (Controlled Practice)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides a worksheet with dotted lines for students to trace simple sentences about food shapes. For example: "This is a round orange." "This is a square cracker." "This is an oval lemon." Below each tracing line, there is a blank line for students to copy the sentence independently. The teacher circulates, offering support with letter formation and spacing.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Fill-in-the-Shape-Word." (Semi-controlled Practice)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a worksheet with pictures of various foods (pineapple, mango, banana, juice box). Under each picture, there is an incomplete sentence like: "This is a ______ (round/square/oval) ______ (food name)." Students choose the correct shape adjective and food noun from a word bank on the board or their worksheet and write it in the blanks. (e.g., "This is a oval banana.")</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: "My Food Sentence Check." (Pair Work/Peer Check)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Each student shows their completed "Fill-in-the-Shape-Word" worksheet to their partner. Partners point to a picture and the written sentence. With teacher modeling, they might say: "You wrote: 'This is a round orange.' Good!" or help identify missing words. The focus is on acknowledging effort in writing and spotting obvious omissions, not on correcting every grammatical error.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual drawing and labeling activity to create "My Shape Snack Plate."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My Shape Snack Plate."</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a paper plate cutout or a drawing of an empty plate.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw 2-3 of their favorite healthy food items from the target vocabulary (e.g., an orange, a cracker, a banana, a slice of mango or papaya, a juice box).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Under each drawn food item, students write a simple phrase to label it, following the model "This is a [shape] [food name]." They can use the words displayed on the board (e.g., "round orange," "square cracker," "oval lemon," "big banana," "healthy mango").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to write independently but provides individual support by pointing to word cards or tracing letters if needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of student "My Shape Snack Plate" drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Shape Snack Plate" drawing with their written labels/sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of written words/labels.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least two target vocabulary words (food names).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted use of shape adjectives (e.g., "round," "square") in labels.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in copying/writing simple phrases.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket by coloring a face or drawing a symbol.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write words about food shapes." (Happy face / Neutral face / Sad face)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I copied sentences today." (Thumbs up / Thumbs sideways / Thumbs down)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food word did you write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write 'square cracker'?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific writing activities were most engaging and effective for 1st graders in practicing sentence formation and labeling? (e.g., tracing, copying, drawing and labeling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with letter formation or spelling of target words (e.g., 'pineapple', 'juice box')? How can I provide more targeted support or scaffolding for these challenges in future writing tasks?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the instructions clear enough for students to understand the writing tasks independently or with minimal guidance?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing lesson helped students practice forming letters and writing simple sentences about food shapes and names. This will be crucial for our next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), where they will integrate all their learned skills (listening, reading, speaking, and writing) to create a collaborative "Healthy Food Shapes" poster for the classroom.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>